  "analysis": "rencrypt is a Python script with three protocols: `rev` (reverse content), `doub` (duplicate content), and `cap` (uppercase). The default is `doub`. Among these, `rev` is the most secure: `doub` literally writes the plaintext twice, `cap` leaves the plaintext readable in uppercase, while `rev` at least reverses the byte order so the content isn't trivially recognizable (marginally more secure, and it's the only reversible transform that doesn't expose plaintext directly). The `-o` flag only accepts a single output file and joins all inputs, so I must invoke rencrypt once per file so each output lands in encrypted_data/ with the same basename.",
  "plan": "1. Create encrypted_data/. 2. Loop over each file in data/ and run `rencrypt -p rev -o encrypted_data/<basename> data/<basename>` so basenames are preserved. 3. Verify the output directory and that contents differ from originals.",
